WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:06.040
This is a pile of paper slips. In about a minute it's going to write a sentence that isn't in the book.

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:16.040
Every slip is a pair of words from a book: the word in the box, and the word that came next. A pair the book says six times gets six slips.

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:29.860
Choose a slip to start from. A story starts after a full stop, so take one with a full stop in the box. This one says "it". Write that word down, then put the slip back.

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:53.080
Now find every slip with "it" in the box. There are ten. Three say "sits", three "watches", three "goes", and one says "is". Shuffle the matching slips. Pick one without looking: "sits". Write it down, then put the slip back. You might need it again.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:01:01.980
Every "sits" slip says "on". And every "on" slip says "the", so there's only one way to go.

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:16.380
"The" has nineteen slips. Pick one: "dog". And "dog" has four: two full stops, two "runs". Pick one: full stop.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:22.700
The book says "It sits on the fence". We've ended up with "It sits on the dog".

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:34.460
After "the", we could pick "magpie", "fence", "postie" or "dog". These four branches show those choices; a thicker branch means more slips.

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:43.140
Run it again and you might get a different sentence, or the same one. That's fine: likely words just come up more often.

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:54.260
Claude and ChatGPT write this way too, one piece at a time. They just look at far more than the last word, and they learn their numbers rather than counting them.

00:01:55.590 --> 00:02:14.600
Spread your cutouts out. Write down a starting word, then find every slip with that word in the box. Shuffle them, pick one without looking, and write down the word beside it. Put the slip back. Then find all the matches for that new word. The colours help you find the matches fast.

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:16.980
Start chaining.
